 The session cookie is also applied to error responses, so a request that throws still slides the window and still persists a session created during it.
 
+## Password Rotation
+
+To rotate `password` without signing users out, move the old one to `extraUnsealPasswords`:
+
+```js
+const session = await useSession(event, {
+  password: "c5b0e1a2-7d3f-4e86-9a14-2f6b8d0c3e57", // new
+  extraUnsealPasswords: ["80d42cfb-1cd2-462c-8f17-e3237d9027e9"], // previous
+});
+```
+
+The extra passwords only **open** sessions. `password` is the only one that seals, so every session written from now on uses it. A cookie session opened with an extra password is resealed with `password` on that same request, so sessions migrate on their own as users come back.
+
+- Keep the list short, most recent first. Passwords are tried in order after `password`.
+- Remove an old password once its sessions have migrated, for example after `maxAge`.
+- Remove a compromised password immediately: while it is listed, it still lets anyone forge valid sessions.
+- Sessions sent through the `x-{name}-session` header cannot be resealed, so they keep depending on the extra password until they expire.
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> Each extra password costs one more PBKDF2 derivation for every cookie that fails verification, so a long list makes invalid or forged cookies more expensive to reject.
+
 ## Use Multiple Sessions
 
 Because each session is stored under its own `name`, you can run several independent sessions on the same request. They live in separate cookies and never overwrite each other, which is useful for keeping unrelated concerns apart, such as a long-lived auth session and a short-lived flash message:
